# Accounts, login and access

**Add accounts** (once per project):

```sh
go tool anetos make:auth
go run . migrate
```

It writes the `User` model, the handlers, [[if .API]]the JSON endpoints under
`/api/v1`[[else]]the pages[[end]], the emails, the routes (`routes/auth.go`), a
migration and tests, and `setupAuth` (in `auth.go`), which `setup` in
`main.go` calls: it returns the `*auth.Auth[*models.User]`, which your
own routes need (keep the value instead of discarding it). Emails go to the
log in development (`MAIL_DRIVER=log`).

**Protect your routes:** put them in a group with the auth middleware
and the login check, as `routes/auth.go` does ([[if .API]]`a.TokenMiddleware`, for
the bearer tokens, then `a.Require`[[else]]`a.Middleware`, after the
session middleware, then `a.Require`[[end]]). In a handler,
`u, ok := auth.User[*models.User](ctx)` is the logged-in user.
[[- if .Web]]

**Login with Google or GitHub:** set `SOCIAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and
`SOCIAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` (or `SOCIAL_GITHUB_…`) in `.env` (see `go tool anetos docs social-login`).
[[- end]]

**Roles and permissions:** declare permissions as constants and check
them with `rbac.Can(ctx, perm)` or guard routes with `rbac.Require`
(`go tool anetos docs guides/roles-and-permissions`). **Policies** for one
record (may this user edit this post?):
`auth.Authorize(ctx, policies.Update, post)`
(`go tool anetos docs authorization`).

Users "log in" and "log out" in code, routes and text. Docs:
`go tool anetos docs [[if .API]]api-accounts[[else]]guides/accounts[[end]]`, `go tool anetos docs guides/authentication`.
Then `go tool anetos check`.
